<h2>The one-line pitch for clients</h2>

<p>"It's the cleanness of a slab door with just enough of a frame to give it some shadow — a picture-frame edge instead of a chunky shaker rail."</p>

<p>That's the gap these two fill. Clients who find a slab too flat and a shaker too traditional — even a slim shaker — now have a door that sits right between them.</p>

<h2>Pacifica: the radius moulding</h2>

<div class="post-images">
  <img src="/images/blog/pacifica-ventura-door-styles/pacifica-deep-water-white-oak.webp" alt="Pacifica flat panel door in Deep Water stain on White Oak, showing the slim rounded applied moulding framing a cathedral-grain oak panel" width="1200" height="1484" loading="lazy" />
</div>

<p><em>Pacifica in Deep Water White Oak.</em></p>

<p>Pacifica frames the panel with a 5/16" applied <strong>radius</strong> moulding on the sides, top, and bottom. The rounded profile catches light along its curve, so the frame reads softly — almost like a piece of furniture or a picture frame rather than a cabinet rail.</p>

<p>The sample above is in <strong>Deep Water</strong>, one of the <a href="/playbook/paint-look-white-oak-stains/">heavy-pigment stains on White Oak</a>. It's a strong pairing: the deep, saturated color reads almost painted from across the room, the oak's cathedral grain telegraphs through on the panel, and the radius edge keeps all that color from feeling heavy.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Best for:</strong> transitional, coastal, and organic-modern kitchens; furniture-style vanities; built-ins and libraries; any client drawn to a softer look who has ruled out traditional shaker.</li>
  <li><strong>Where it wins:</strong> statement islands and color-forward runs, where the rounded profile makes a saturated stain feel approachable.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Ventura: the square moulding</h2>

<div class="post-images">
  <img src="/images/blog/pacifica-ventura-door-styles/ventura-warm-stone-white-oak.webp" alt="Ventura flat panel door in Warm Stone stain on White Oak, showing the slim square applied moulding framing a straight-grain oak panel" width="1200" height="1331" loading="lazy" />
</div>

<p><em>Ventura in Warm Stone White Oak.</em></p>

<p>Ventura uses the same 5/16" applied moulding width, but in a <strong>square</strong> profile. The edge is a crisp, hard line — a thin architectural reveal around the panel that reads as modern and precise.</p>

<p>The sample is in <strong>Warm Stone</strong>, the soft, warm cream from the same White Oak stain program. The grain shows through as a subtle texture and the square frame draws a fine outline around it: a light, quiet door that still has more depth than a flat slab.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Best for:</strong> modern, minimalist, Japandi, and warm-contemporary kitchens; projects where a slab feels too plain but anything with a radius feels too traditional.</li>
  <li><strong>Where it wins:</strong> full-kitchen perimeters in a light finish, tall pantry walls, and baths — anywhere you want a calm, tailored surface with a precise edge.</li>
</ul>

