<h2>Spec notes before you quote</h2>

<p>These rules apply to both styles. Read them before you put either one in front of a client — a couple of them change how you design the kitchen.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Doors and drawer fronts are not grain matched.</strong> If the client expects grain flowing continuously from a drawer stack into the door below, set that expectation now — or steer them toward a grain-matched program like the one in our <a href="/playbook/grain-matched-white-oak-metro-straw/">Metro white oak walkthrough</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Doors are vertical grain only.</strong> Drawer fronts can be ordered vertical or horizontal. On a horizontal-grain drawer bank next to vertical-grain doors, show the client a sample of both orientations together.</li>
  <li><strong>Glass frame doors are different construction.</strong> Specify <strong>GFD</strong>. They're built as a 5-piece hardwood frame with <strong>2 1/8" wide rails</strong> — much wider than the 5/16" moulding on the solid doors. Glass prep and installed glass clips are no charge, but the wider frame will look different next to the solid doors, so show that in the drawings.</li>
  <li><strong>No mullion doors.</strong> Neither style is available as a mullion door.</li>
  <li><strong>No edge profiles.</strong> The moulding is the detail; there are no edge profile options on either the door or drawer front.</li>
  <li><strong>No rustic species.</strong> Neither style is available in rustic species.</li>
  <li><strong>Rail tolerance:</strong> door rails may vary ±1/16" due to the manufacturing process.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why these two are worth a spot in your showroom</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>They fill a real gap.</strong> Slab, slim shaker, and shaker cover most clients, but there's a growing group who want something in between. Pacifica and Ventura are built for exactly that conversation.</li>
  <li><strong>They make the White Oak stains look their best.</strong> A flat veneered panel is the best canvas for grain, and a thin applied frame adds shadow without competing with it. Deep Water and Warm Stone both read richer on these doors than on a busier style.</li>
  <li><strong>One display, two stories.</strong> Show the two side by side and you've demonstrated how a single profile change takes the same door from soft to sharp. That's an easy way to get a client to tell you which direction they lean.</li>
</ul>

